<Layout title="About Cosmin Ghinoiu | Client Delivery, Implementation and AI" description="Nine years at Copyright Clearance Center delivering enterprise software to pharma and life-sciences clients, 50+ projects since 2022, and an independent production AI service since October 2026." current="/about/">
  <section class="about-intro section-wrap"><div><h1>I’m Cosmin. <br />I get complicated <br />delivery to work.</h1><a class="text-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/cosmin-ghinoiu/" rel="me">Connect on LinkedIn <Arrow direction="up-right" /></a></div><div class="about-opening"><p>I do my best work when delivery is complicated, with technical dependencies, competing priorities, and teams that see the same problem differently.</p><p>In those situations I work out what needs to happen, turn it into a plan people can follow, and keep everyone on that plan until it’s done.</p><p>That’s the work I’m looking for now, in client delivery, implementation and service delivery roles, permanent or contract, or as a defined project.</p></div></section>
  <section class="about-background section-wrap"><h2>From technical detail <br />to client delivery.</h2><div class="prose"><p>I’ve worked in technical support, client engagement and enterprise implementation. I spent nine years at Copyright Clearance Center, starting as the first Romania-based support specialist for its enterprise product and finishing as Lead Client Service Delivery Manager, mostly for global pharma and life-sciences clients. Because of that path I understand both how the software actually works and what the people introducing, using and supporting it need from it.</p><p>Between 2022 and July 2026 I led 50+ specialized delivery projects, including implementations, integrations, migrations, technical reviews and workflow redesigns, and I was the implementation and service manager for five strategic accounts worth over $2M a year in recurring licences. Every account I managed renewed, and the contractual service levels for availability, order fulfilment and support response were met throughout. I’ve delivered over 40 API integrations, from discovery and approvals through configuration and the operational support that keeps them within the agreed terms.</p><p>I’ve also run more than 100 live user and administrator training sessions, the largest for 400+ people. Training has been part of my delivery work the whole time, from explaining a single configuration to preparing a team to run the platform on their own.</p><a class="text-link" href="/contact/">Request my current CV <Arrow /></a>
    <div class="about-certifications" id="certifications">
      <h3>Certifications</h3>
      <ul role="list">
        <li>Project Management Professional (PMP), 2026</li>
        <li>PRINCE2 Agile Foundation</li>
        <li>ITIL 4 Foundation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="about-ai section-wrap" id="working-with-ai"><h2>Working <br />with AI.</h2><div class="prose"><p>On the client side, I’ve delivered two enterprise AI rollouts: a 12-month pilot that went to a 20,000-user launch, and a second client’s AI search and summarization. In both, my part was the delivery and the evaluation. I tested answer quality with the client, worked with the engineers on guardrails and retrieval settings, and kept everything inside the content licences. The engineers built the models and the pipelines.</p><p>Since October 2026 I’ve also been running SuppClaimCheck, a production AI service that checks supplement claims against open-access literature. I designed it, I direct the Claude Code agents that write the code under rules I set, I review every change, I run the evals and I operate it. I used the same discipline as on the client rollouts (grounded answers, evaluation before release, constraints written down and applied in code), but here I own every decision. Day to day I work with Claude, Claude Code, Codex and ChatGPT for research, documentation and analysis.</p><a class="text-link" href="/portfolio/#suppclaimcheck">Read about SuppClaimCheck <Arrow /></a></div></section>
  <section class="working-style section-wrap" id="working-practices"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How I work.</h2><p>I use just enough structure for people to get the work done, <br class="desktop-break" /> and I follow through on what we agreed.</p></div><div class="working-rows"><article><h3>Start with the real work</h3><p>I work through the requirements, constraints and current workflows first, and only then settle on a delivery approach, so the plan fits the situation.</p></article><article><h3>Make ownership visible</h3><p>I check what the business is asking for against what the software can actually do, bring in the people who need to decide, and keep a visible list of who owns each action and what it depends on.</p></article><article><h3>Leave useful knowledge behind</h3><p>During employment, I led a weekly peer knowledge-sharing program over one year and helped develop reusable delivery and review frameworks. I put the same care into training, documentation and handover.</p></article><article><h3>Check it with data before trusting it</h3><p>On one account I went through more than a million user-action records with the client to work out which departments needed basic and which needed advanced training. On client AI rollouts I tested answer quality with the users before each phase. On my own service, every change runs against a labelled eval set before it can go live.</p></article></div></section>
